## (1) Root-cause reading — CORRECT

- `support/lit` wraps the payload as `{:type :literal :value x}` (`test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_support.cljc:47`); `load-ast` (:223) projects via `project` (:40) into `linearize/project-rows`, which throws `Cannot project rows that fail validation` at `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/linearize.cljc:204` — matches the lane error.
- Literal grammar `:literal [[:value :data]]` (`src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:1065`); slot check `(:data :key) (plain-data? v)` (`vm.cljc:1317`).
- `plain-data?` (`vm.cljc:965`) accepts scalars via `(boolean? number? cbor/float64? string? keyword? symbol?)`. Per `integer-host/big-carrier?` (`src/cljc/yin/vm/integer/host.cljc:41-48`), JS and Dart BigInt answer **false** to `number?`, so the payload's big integers fail the map-values walk; on the JVM `clojure.lang.BigInt` is a `java.lang.Number`, so `number?` admits it — exactly the observed host asymmetry.
- The `{:path [1]}` defect is the literal's first grammar slot: `validate-rows` numbers slots with `(range 1 (inc (count slots)))` over `(rest (subvec row 1))` (`vm.clc:1432-1435`) — 1-based, tag excluded. The report's rejection of the "second float in the vector" reading is right.
